{"author":{"id":"a66e0db0201c515c5090d5e52c036411dda9ba2f138110a926616a18a8ea0dd6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790684611,"nonce":"lsLXGsKVcXtNOHLD3kJuCayBr1I=","message":"Rayzor's existing `gpu` crate now includes the generated xgpu model and shared backend instead of exposing a parallel package.\n\n- The adapter supplies Rayzor Text, Bytes, error, root and externally completable Future carriers. Generated descriptors and runtime symbols are merged with the existing `rayzor-gpu` package table.\n- `generate_rayzor_with_resources` lets Rayzor own the `GpuBuffer` wrapper while generated portable methods still use xgpu's integer handle. This preserves lazy-graph metadata on the same language object.\n- xgpu retains the native device/queue association for buffers in a side table. `GPUCompute.fromDevice(GpuDevice)` clones the same wgpu handles, so portable commands, `@:shader`, lazy/fused compute and specialized kernels share ordering and native resources without copying.\n- Rayzor's old graphics and compute code now builds on wgpu 30. Both the native adapter and `wasm32-unknown-unknown` browser-host build pass.\n- Rayzor release generation merges the portable `GpuBuffer` extern with its compute/operator methods and packages the complete generated tree in `rayzor-gpu.rpkg`. A catalog regression test rejects duplicate class/method registrations.\n\nValidation: xgpu workspace tests pass (21 bindgen, 6 core, wire/browser tests); Caribou GPU tests pass (8); Rayzor GPU tests pass (52 with the adapter catalog test); native and wasm32 Rayzor GPU checks pass.\n\nThe remaining cross-repository release step is to commit xgpu, replace the temporary sibling path dependencies with one pinned Git revision, and run Rayzor's release-package CI from that pin. Ash remains tracked by its Future/hl_abi prerequisite.","files":null}]}